<h1>Washdown Conveyors: IP Ratings, Materials &amp; Common Problems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A washdown conveyor is designed to withstand direct water, foam, and chemical cleaning without damage to motors, bearings, or electrical components. Washdown conveyors are standard in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage</a>, <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a>, and other industries requiring daily wet sanitation. Key features include stainless steel construction, sealed components (IP65-IP69K), and sloped surfaces that shed water.</p>
<p>This page covers washdown-rated equipment: component ratings, materials, and the <a href="#problems-heading">common washdown conveyor problems</a> that shorten service life. For hygienic design standards (USDA, 3-A, surface finish), see <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyors</a>. For belt washers, scrapers, and CIP spray equipment, see <a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">conveyor cleaning systems</a>.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/washdown-dorner-aquapruf-7400-ultimate.webp" width="1000" height="651" alt="End view of a stainless steel conveyor with a white modular plastic belt, an open frame, and white rollers at the belt end." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Dorner AquaPruf 7400 Ultimate conveyor: stainless steel open frame and plastic modular belt, the typical construction for conveyors cleaned with direct water spray. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/aquapruf/aquapruf-7400-ultimate" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="ip-heading">IP Ratings for Washdown</h2>
<p>IP (Ingress Protection) ratings, defined in <a href="https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/2452" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IEC 60529</a>, describe the level of protection against dust and water. The K-suffix ratings such as IP69K come from ISO 20653, a road-vehicle standard that food equipment suppliers adopted for high-pressure washdown testing. The first digit indicates dust protection (6 = dust-tight). The second digit indicates water protection. All washdown conveyor components (motors, bearings, sensors, enclosures) should be rated appropriately for the cleaning method used.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Rating</th>
<th>Water Protection</th>
<th>Cleaning Method</th>
<th>Application</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>IP65</td>
<td>Protected against water jets from any direction</td>
<td>Low-pressure hose, hand spray</td>
<td>Light washdown, wipe-down with wet cloths</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>IP66</td>
<td>Protected against powerful water jets</td>
<td>Pressure washer, direct spray</td>
<td>Standard food plant washdown</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>IP67</td>
<td>Protected against temporary immersion (1m, 30min)</td>
<td>Flooding, deep cleaning</td>
<td>Conveyors that may be submerged briefly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>IP69K</td>
<td>Protected against close-range high-pressure, high-temperature spray (80°C water at 80 to 100 bar, per <a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ip69k-ingress-protection-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Keystone Compliance</a>)</td>
<td>Hot high-pressure spray</td>
<td>Heavy washdown in meat, poultry, dairy</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> For most <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage applications</a>, specify IP66 minimum for all components. Where crews use hot, high-pressure spray, specify IP69K. IP69K is a test rating, not a sanitary certification: it does not by itself show that equipment meets <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">USDA or 3-A hygienic design</a> requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Light vs. heavy washdown:</strong> "Heavy washdown" is an industry term, not a standardized rating. It usually describes daily cleaning with high-pressure spray, hot water, and caustic or chlorinated foam, as in meat, poultry, and dairy plants. A heavy washdown conveyor needs IP69K-rated motors, sensors, and bearings, all-stainless or compatible polymer construction, and a material check against the plant's actual cleaning chemicals. Light washdown (low-pressure hose, mild detergent) can usually be served by IP65 or IP66 components. Ask suppliers to state the cleaning pressure, temperature, and chemistry their equipment is rated for, not only an IP code.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading">Washdown Conveyor Materials</h2>
<h3>Frame Materials</h3>
<p><strong>304 Stainless Steel</strong> is the standard for most food and pharmaceutical washdown conveyors. It resists corrosion from water, mild detergents, and most food acids. All welds should be ground smooth and passivated to prevent corrosion initiation points.</p>
<p><strong>316 Stainless Steel</strong> (or 316L) provides superior corrosion resistance for harsh chemicals, high chloride environments (salt, cheese brine), and acidic products. <a href="https://www.ssina.com/education/corrosion/pitting-and-crevice-corrosion/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSINA identifies chlorides</a> as the usual cause of pitting and crevice corrosion in stainless steel, and molybdenum (present in 316, absent in 304) as a key alloying element against it. 316 is the usual choice for seafood, brine, and chloride-heavy sanitation programs. It costs more than 304.</p>
<p><strong>Painted or galvanized steel</strong> is generally avoided in washdown zones. Paint chips can contaminate products. Zinc coatings corrode under repeated wet cleaning and caustic chemicals.</p>
<h3>Belting Materials</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Polyurethane (PU):</strong> FDA-compliant, oil-resistant, easy to clean, good for dry and lightly wet foods</li>
<li><strong>Modular plastic:</strong> Polypropylene (PP), acetal (POM), or polyethylene (PE) modules with hinge rods. Excellent for wet environments, tool-free module replacement. See <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular conveyors</a></li>
<li><strong>Wire mesh:</strong> Stainless steel wire for high-temperature baking, cooling, and frying. Open weave allows water drainage. See <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh conveyors</a></li>
</ul>
<h3>Component Materials</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bearings:</strong> Stainless steel housings with food-grade H1 grease. Sealed (2RS) or IP69K-rated units</li>
<li><strong>Fasteners:</strong> All stainless steel. No zinc-plated or galvanized hardware</li>
<li><strong>Motors:</strong> Washdown-rated (TEFC or TENV), stainless steel or epoxy-coated housing, IP66/IP69K</li>
<li><strong>Sensors:</strong> Stainless steel body, IP67 or IP69K rated</li>
<li><strong>Enclosures:</strong> NEMA Type 4X (stainless) for electrical components. <a href="https://www.nema.org/docs/default-source/products-document-library/nema-enclosure-types.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NEMA defines Type 4X</a> as protecting against hose-directed water with an additional level of protection against corrosion</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="design-heading">Washdown Design Features</h2>
<h3>Sloped Surfaces</h3>
<p>Horizontal surfaces should be sloped to shed water, with the slope set by the hygienic design guideline the plant follows. Frame tops, motor mounts, sensor brackets, and any surface that could collect water must slope toward drainage. Standing water breeds bacteria and accelerates corrosion.</p>
<h3>Open Frame Construction</h3>
<p>Open tubular frames allow water to drain and air to circulate for drying. Avoid box-frame designs with enclosed cavities that trap water. All frame members should have drain holes at low points. Round or oval tubing is easier to clean than square tubing.</p>
<h3>Smooth Welds</h3>
<p>All welds should be ground smooth and passivated (chemically treated to restore the protective oxide layer). Rough welds and crevices harbor bacteria. For <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">full sanitary design</a>, specify continuous welded construction with polished surfaces.</p>
<h3>Tool-Free Belt Access</h3>
<p>Quick-release belt tensioners and lift-out belt supports allow tool-free belt removal for cleaning. This reduces cleaning time and encourages thorough sanitation. <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a> allow individual module replacement without removing the entire belt.</p>
<h3>Component Accessibility</h3>
<p>Motors, drives, and electrical components should be accessible for cleaning without disassembly. Mount components away from direct spray zones when possible. Use quick-disconnect electrical connections to simplify cleaning around control panels.</p>
<h2 id="sanitary-heading">Washdown vs. Sanitary</h2>
<p>The terms "washdown" and "sanitary" are often used interchangeably but have different meanings:</p>
<p><strong>Washdown conveyors</strong> are designed to survive water and chemical cleaning without damage. The focus is on protecting components (motors, bearings, electronics) from water intrusion. A conveyor can be washdown-rated without being fully sanitary.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary conveyors</a></strong> go further with hygienic design principles that prevent bacterial harborage: smooth continuous welds, no horizontal surfaces, open frame construction, tool-free belt removal, radius corners instead of sharp angles, and design to USDA, 3-A, or EHEDG hygienic guidelines where required. For 3-A, the <a href="https://www.3-a.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A Symbol</a> requires third-party verification by a Certified Conformance Evaluator, so ask for the certificate. All sanitary conveyors are washdown-rated, but not all washdown conveyors meet full sanitary standards.</p>
<h4>When to Specify Full Sanitary</h4>
<ul>
<li>Direct food contact (open product exposure)</li>
<li>USDA-inspected plant or 3-A Symbol required</li>
<li>Ready-to-eat (RTE) products</li>
<li>Dairy, meat, poultry, or seafood processing</li>
<li>Pharmaceutical or medical device manufacturing</li>
</ul>
<p>For packaged products, cased goods, or post-seal handling, washdown rating without full sanitary design may be acceptable.</p>
<h2 id="cleaning-heading">Cleaning Protocols</h2>
<h3>Standard Washdown Sequence</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pre-rinse:</strong> Remove loose debris with low-pressure water</li>
<li><strong>Foam application:</strong> Apply foaming detergent, allow contact time (per chemical instructions)</li>
<li><strong>Scrub:</strong> Brush or agitate stubborn soils if needed</li>
<li><strong>Rinse:</strong> High-pressure rinse to remove detergent and suspended debris</li>
<li><strong>Sanitize:</strong> Apply sanitizer (quaternary ammonium, chlorine, or peracetic acid)</li>
<li><strong>Final rinse:</strong> Rinse sanitizer if required by chemical type</li>
<li><strong>Dry:</strong> Air dry, air knife, or towel dry before production restart</li>
</ol>
<h3>Clean-in-Place (CIP)</h3>
<p>Automated CIP systems use fixed spray nozzles to deliver cleaning chemicals without manual intervention. CIP is common in dairy, beverage, and pharmaceutical applications. Conveyors designed for CIP must ensure all belt surfaces receive adequate spray coverage and that chemicals drain completely after cleaning. See <a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">conveyor cleaning systems</a> for detailed CIP information.</p>
<h3>Drying</h3>
<p>Drying after washdown is critical to prevent bacterial regrowth. Water left on conveyor surfaces supports microbial growth and biofilm formation. Air knives, blowers, or manual drying with clean towels remove residual water. Some operations use conveyor heaters or infrared dryers for faster drying.</p>
<h2 id="problems-heading">Common Washdown Conveyor Problems</h2>
<p>Most washdown conveyor failures trace back to a mismatch between the equipment and the sanitation program: components rated for a lighter cleaning method than the plant actually uses, or construction details that trap water and chemicals. Use the problems below as a checklist when writing a spec or reviewing a failing line.</p>
<h3>1. Premature Bearing Failure</h3>
<p><strong>Cause:</strong> High-pressure spray forces water and cleaning chemicals past bearing seals and washes out grease. Corrosion and loss of lubrication follow, often within a shorter interval than the bearing's rated life.</p>
<p><strong>Design fix:</strong> Specify sealed stainless or thermoplastic housings with washdown-rated seals and NSF H1 food-grade grease. Consider sealed-for-life or polymer bearings where relubrication is impractical. Train sanitation crews not to aim spray directly at seals, and relubricate after washdown if the bearing supplier calls for it.</p>
<h3>2. Water Intrusion into Motors</h3>
<p><strong>Cause:</strong> The motor or gearmotor is rated below the cleaning method (for example, IP65 under hot high-pressure spray), cable glands or conduit entries are damaged or loose, or a warm motor draws in moist air as it cools after a cold rinse.</p>
<p><strong>Design fix:</strong> Match the IP rating to the real cleaning method, using IP69K where hot high-pressure spray is used. Specify washdown-duty motors (often TENV with stainless or coated housings), sealed stainless cable glands, and conduit entries that face down. Ask the motor supplier how the unit manages condensation.</p>
<h3>3. Harborage Points</h3>
<p><strong>Cause:</strong> Hollow or box-section frames, lap joints, skip welds, exposed threads, and flat ledges hold water and residue that routine cleaning cannot reach. Swab results stay positive even after a full washdown.</p>
<p><strong>Design fix:</strong> Use open frames with continuous welds, sloped surfaces, standoffs instead of flush-mounted brackets, and no fasteners in product zones. These are core <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor design</a> principles. Apply them wherever the product is exposed.</p>
<h3>4. Belt Tracking and Slip in Wet Conditions</h3>
<p><strong>Cause:</strong> A wet belt loses friction on a smooth drive pulley and slips or wanders. Residue on pulleys, belt tension released for cleaning and not reset the same way, and thermal expansion or contraction of plastic belts after hot washdown all make tracking worse.</p>
<p><strong>Design fix:</strong> Use positive-drive belting (<a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular plastic</a> or sprocket-driven thermoplastic) in wet zones. Where friction-driven flat belts are needed, specify tensioners with repeatable set points, crowned or lagged pulleys, and clean pulleys as part of the sanitation procedure.</p>
<h3>5. Chemical Attack on Materials</h3>
<p><strong>Cause:</strong> Chlorinated sanitizers and salt brines can pit 304 stainless steel. Caustic cleaners attack aluminum. Some plastics, belt compounds, and seal elastomers degrade under certain chemicals, concentrations, or temperatures. Zinc-plated and galvanized hardware corrode quickly.</p>
<p><strong>Design fix:</strong> Give suppliers the plant's actual cleaning chemicals, concentrations, and temperatures, and check them against each supplier's chemical compatibility data for the frame, belt, seals, and fasteners. Specify 316 stainless where chlorides are present, and avoid aluminum and plated hardware in the washdown zone.</p>
