<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a washdown conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A washdown conveyor is designed to withstand direct water, foam, and chemical cleaning without damage to motors, bearings, or electrical components. Features include <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel construction</a>, sealed bearings (IP65-IP69K), washdown-rated motors, NEMA 4X enclosures, and sloped surfaces that shed water. Washdown conveyors are standard in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a> and <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a> applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What IP rating is required for washdown conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Match the rating to the cleaning method. IP65 covers water jets and suits low-pressure hose-down. IP66 covers powerful water jets and suits direct spray cleaning. IP69K, defined in ISO 20653 rather than IEC 60529, covers close-range high-pressure, high-temperature spray and is commonly specified for heavy washdown in meat, poultry, and dairy plants. IP69K is a test rating, not a sanitary certification, so it does not by itself mean equipment meets USDA or 3-A hygienic design requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What materials are used in washdown conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Frames are 304 stainless steel (general food) or 316 stainless steel (harsh chemicals, salt). Belting is FDA-compliant PU, <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular plastic</a>, or <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">stainless wire mesh</a>. Bearings use stainless housings with food-grade grease. All fasteners are stainless steel. Painted or galvanized steel is generally avoided in washdown zones.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the difference between washdown and sanitary conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Washdown conveyors survive water and chemical cleaning. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary conveyors</a> go further with hygienic design: smooth welds, no horizontal surfaces, open frames, tool-free belt removal, and design to USDA, 3-A, or EHEDG hygienic guidelines where required. All sanitary conveyors are washdown-rated, but not all washdown conveyors meet full sanitary standards.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What are the most common washdown conveyor problems?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The most common problems are premature bearing failure from water and chemicals washing out grease, water intrusion into motors rated below the cleaning method, harborage points in hollow frames and lap joints, belt slip and mistracking on wet friction-driven belts, and chemical attack on 304 stainless, aluminum, plastics, or seals. Most trace back to components rated for a lighter cleaning program than the plant actually runs. See <a href="#problems-heading">common washdown conveyor problems</a> for causes and design fixes.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-6">How much do washdown conveyors cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-6" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Washdown conveyors cost more than equivalent painted steel conveyors because of stainless steel construction, sealed bearings, washdown-duty motors, and rated electrical components. The premium grows with the IP rating, with 316 instead of 304 stainless, and with full sanitary design or USDA acceptance. Width, length, belt type, and controls also drive price. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for details.</p>

</div>
</div>
